    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d125">

                <pb facs="1148" n="990" sameAs="frus:frus1906p2/pg990"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Japanese
                    Chargé</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1906-04-10"><hi rendition="#italic">April 10,
                        1906</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 265.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to Mr. Takahira’s note No.
                        39,<anchor n="a" xml:id="d125-f1"/> and to your note No. 42,<anchor n="b"
                        xml:id="d125-f2"/> dated, respectively, July 20 and July 29, 1905,
                    requesting that certain violations of the Geneva convention of 1864 and The
                    Hague convention of 1899, by Russian troops, be brought to the attention of the
                    Russian Government, I have the honor to inclose herewith, for your information,
                    a copy of a dispatch<anchor n="c" xml:id="d125-f3"/> from the American
                    ambassador to Russia inclosing a copy of a note from the Russian Government, in
                    answer to both of the Japanese notes.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Bacon.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
